(6668306, 996809, 43463, '<td valign="top">Voted earlier, coming back to comment now.<br/> <br/>Would a real rabbit have been better? If going for a bit more ''realism'', yes. BUT, the stuffed animal rabbit <b>is perfect</b> when playing into the concepts of childhood, innocence and magic. It makes me think of childhood and being captivated and enchanted by watching a magician pull the rabbit or dove out of the hat. Hmmm, or mayhap there is another enchanting idea you are trying to convey. Where do loved stuffed animals go when no longer wanted or needed? Perhaps they travel down the ''rabbit hole'' in the magic hat to a land akin to Alice in Wonderland where they become ''real''. The lighing is well done in the composition. The magic smoke and sparkles arise out of the hat to cement the idea that this hat is a door to an enchanted realm/home. Nicely done!</td>', 'Voted earlier, coming back to comment now. Would a real rabbit have been better? If going for a bit more ''realism'', yes. BUT, the stuffed animal rabbit is perfect when playing into the concepts of childhood, innocence and magic. It makes me think of childhood and being captivated and enchanted by watching a magician pull the rabbit or dove out of the hat. Hmmm, or mayhap there is another enchanting idea you are trying to convey. Where do loved stuffed animals go when no longer wanted or needed? Perhaps they travel down the ''rabbit hole'' in the magic hat to a land akin to Alice in Wonderland where they become ''real''. The lighing is well done in the composition. The magic smoke and sparkles arise out of the hat to cement the idea that this hat is a door to an enchanted realm/home. Nicely done!', '2012-02-09 13:34:14.000000', NULL, 1),
(6668311, 996719, 43463, '<td valign="top">Voted earlier coming back to comment now.<br/> <br/>A very interesting take on the dryad/tree nympths mythos. I like how you have a house off in the distance for it is a subtle visual clue that this is "home". The color red is vibrant - symbolically it makes me think of ''life''s blood'' - and indeed the hair or roots are this creature''s life blood. It is her connection to mother earth. There are two things that I think would make the composition more stronger. First the blending of the tree base to the ground doesn''t look well integrated or seamlessly connected to the ground. I would try selecting that tree base from the original image and feathering the selection more ....100 pixels or so. Or using the clone tool with an opacity of 60 -90 (trial and error), choose the spot you want to clone, and with a curved downward motion ''brush'' the corners into the scene. Lastly, I think her arms are in an awkward position. The palms of her hands are up towards the sky when I think it would be more effective if they were pointed down towards the earth. That is where her ''roots'' are so to speak, so she should be reaching in that direction rather than the sky. Still, a well done composition and I think it should do well.</td>', 'Voted earlier coming back to comment now. A very interesting take on the dryad/tree nympths mythos. I like how you have a house off in the distance for it is a subtle visual clue that this is "home". The color red is vibrant - symbolically it makes me think of ''life''s blood'' - and indeed the hair or roots are this creature''s life blood. It is her connection to mother earth. There are two things that I think would make the composition more stronger. First the blending of the tree base to the ground doesn''t look well integrated or seamlessly connected to the ground. I would try selecting that tree base from the original image and feathering the selection more ....100 pixels or so. Or using the clone tool with an opacity of 60 -90 (trial and error), choose the spot you want to clone, and with a curved downward motion ''brush'' the corners into the scene. Lastly, I think her arms are in an awkward position. The palms of her hands are up towards the sky when I think it would be more effective if they were pointed down towards the earth. That is where her ''roots'' are so to speak, so she should be reaching in that direction rather than the sky. Still, a well done composition and I think it should do well.', '2012-02-09 13:46:44.000000', NULL, 1),
(6668338, 995855, 43463, '<td valign="top">Voted earlier coming back to comment.<br/> <br/>I love that castle! It is a nice element to an enchanted forest scene....however, there is just something odd to it. With that big portcullis entrance I''d figure that it has open to a bigger courtyard. Looking closer it looks like you only included a portion in the image which is a shame because it is and should be your prime focal point. Play up the strengths - show it all! There are some other things that could boost the visual interest of the scene. First off - be careful of size differences. The common thinking is castles are big. Those leaves in the foreground are almost the same size as the castle. To make you audience believe that this is a fairy castle you have to put an element into the composition that would suspend our belief the castle is huge. A small person with wings walking up the walkway would do it. Or if not a small castle the leaves should be to scale to the relative size of what it would be in reality in comparision to the castle. Second, colors can be a strength or weakness to a composition. For me, I am getting a color overload of orange/brown. It doesn''t allow the castle to visual pop in the scene. The castle ''becomes'' part of the riot of browns and orange hues that dominate the scene. I wonder if making it B&amp;W would have been better so that it would instead allow the eye to look at the contrasts between the elements (light and shadow) so that the castle takes a more forward role in the imagery.</td>', 'Voted earlier coming back to comment. I love that castle! It is a nice element to an enchanted forest scene....however, there is just something odd to it. With that big portcullis entrance I''d figure that it has open to a bigger courtyard. Looking closer it looks like you only included a portion in the image which is a shame because it is and should be your prime focal point. Play up the strengths - show it all! There are some other things that could boost the visual interest of the scene. First off - be careful of size differences. The common thinking is castles are big. Those leaves in the foreground are almost the same size as the castle. To make you audience believe that this is a fairy castle you have to put an element into the composition that would suspend our belief the castle is huge. A small person with wings walking up the walkway would do it. Or if not a small castle the leaves should be to scale to the relative size of what it would be in reality in comparision to the castle. Second, colors can be a strength or weakness to a composition. For me, I am getting a color overload of orange/brown. It doesn''t allow the castle to visual pop in the scene. The castle ''becomes'' part of the riot of browns and orange hues that dominate the scene. I wonder if making it B&W would have been better so that it would instead allow the eye to look at the contrasts between the elements (light and shadow) so that the castle takes a more forward role in the imagery.', '2012-02-09 14:59:58.000000', NULL, 1),
(6668365, 996794, 43463, '<td valign="top">Voted earlier, coming back to leave comments.<br/> <br/>''Hats'' off you to for the humor (pun intended):-) I am not convinced that the desaturation of all elements except the banana really work. The image has potential but needs some work. First off I think it would have been better to have a black background for the white of the ''gloves'' and the magician''s sleeves to pop off the backdrop. The challenge would be to light the black hat so it does not blend into the backdrop. Easy enough with some small lights (flashlights or small desk spotlight) that can be hidden behind the hat. It''s best to make sure that you have some distance between wall and hat (say 3 feet) and hat and light (7 to 14 inches). This will help illuminate the hat by ''outlining'' the edges; separating it from the black backdrop. You will also have to have a light on the front of the hat and magician to keep lighting balanced. There is a lot of ''noise'' or grain in the shot. Shooting with a faster ISO introduces that noise. The options are to shoot with a lower ISO 200 or less OR run the image through a Denoise program such as Topaz Denoise or Noise Ninja. Lastly, when composing your image make sure that you have all the necessary elements to complete the ''illusion''. The rubber gloves just don''t speak of a magician. It ruins the imagery of a young, inexperienced magician being frustrated at not mastering the magic of the hat.</td>', 'Voted earlier, coming back to leave comments. ''Hats'' off you to for the humor (pun intended):-) I am not convinced that the desaturation of all elements except the banana really work. The image has potential but needs some work. First off I think it would have been better to have a black background for the white of the ''gloves'' and the magician''s sleeves to pop off the backdrop. The challenge would be to light the black hat so it does not blend into the backdrop. Easy enough with some small lights (flashlights or small desk spotlight) that can be hidden behind the hat. It''s best to make sure that you have some distance between wall and hat (say 3 feet) and hat and light (7 to 14 inches). This will help illuminate the hat by ''outlining'' the edges; separating it from the black backdrop. You will also have to have a light on the front of the hat and magician to keep lighting balanced. There is a lot of ''noise'' or grain in the shot. Shooting with a faster ISO introduces that noise. The options are to shoot with a lower ISO 200 or less OR run the image through a Denoise program such as Topaz Denoise or Noise Ninja. Lastly, when composing your image make sure that you have all the necessary elements to complete the ''illusion''. The rubber gloves just don''t speak of a magician. It ruins the imagery of a young, inexperienced magician being frustrated at not mastering the magic of the hat.', '2012-02-09 15:50:52.000000', NULL, 1),
(6668372, 996760, 108580, '<td valign="top">This is stunning. It''s exactly the location I''d choose to give birth to a sphere :) It can''t roll away!. The post processing, light, shade, shadows, and cloud/sun vortex are stunning. The kiss of light (rimlight) on the fore priest and reduced kiss of light on the rear priests is masterful. Good luck.</td>', 'This is stunning. It''s exactly the location I''d choose to give birth to a sphere :) It can''t roll away!. The post processing, light, shade, shadows, and cloud/sun vortex are stunning. The kiss of light (rimlight) on the fore priest and reduced kiss of light on the rear priests is masterful. Good luck.', '2012-02-09 15:57:18.000000', NULL, 1),
(6668374, 996741, 43463, '<td valign="top">Voted earlier, coming back to comment now.<br/> <br/>I am failing to see what I feel you want your audience to see &amp; experience. I see clearly the watch with the time, the table set with food, and the empty shoes off to the far left. But, I can''t see what is in the middle center of the image - I feel that an element that is pertinent to the image is there that would give the composition further meaning. It''s just too dark - I looked on my monitor on my PC and later on a laptop which is brighter...but I see nothing:-( It''s just too dark in that area to make out any solid details. My guess is that some people gathered around the table to eat, received a call, and ''poof'', vanished leaving shoes and whatever else I cannot see behind. Or maybe I am just missing the point entirely. On technicals - the conversion to B&amp;W is good with very dynamic tones between light and shadow (with the exception of the problem area that needs more illumination)</td>', 'Voted earlier, coming back to comment now. I am failing to see what I feel you want your audience to see & experience. I see clearly the watch with the time, the table set with food, and the empty shoes off to the far left. But, I can''t see what is in the middle center of the image - I feel that an element that is pertinent to the image is there that would give the composition further meaning. It''s just too dark - I looked on my monitor on my PC and later on a laptop which is brighter...but I see nothing:-( It''s just too dark in that area to make out any solid details. My guess is that some people gathered around the table to eat, received a call, and ''poof'', vanished leaving shoes and whatever else I cannot see behind. Or maybe I am just missing the point entirely. On technicals - the conversion to B&W is good with very dynamic tones between light and shadow (with the exception of the problem area that needs more illumination)', '2012-02-09 16:00:55.000000', NULL, 1),
